# Bug Found: Off-by-One Error in Loop Condition

## The Defect

In the loop condition:

```typescript
for (let i = 0; i <= values.length; i++) {
    total += values[i];
}
```

The use of `i <= values.length` causes the loop to iterate one time beyond the valid array indices. Since arrays are zero-indexed, the last valid index is `values.length - 1`. Accessing `values[values.length]` returns `undefined`, which results in `NaN` when added to `total`.

## Corrected Code

```typescript
function average(values: number[]) {
    let total = 0;

    for (let i = 0; i < values.length; i++) {  // Fixed: use < instead of <=
        total += values[i];
    }

    return total / values.length;
}
```

## Why This Matters

- **Incorrect condition (`<=`)**: Loop runs `values.length + 1` times, accessing an invalid index.
- **Correct condition (`<`)**: Loop runs exactly `values.length` times, covering all valid indices.

## Example Demonstration

